---
import SubpageLayout from '../../layouts/SubpageLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import FeatureRow from '../../components/FeatureRow.astro';
import CoverageMap from '../../components/CoverageMap.astro';
import RateLookup from '../../components/RateLookup.astro';
import { absolute } from '../../lib/seo';
import { lastmodFor } from '../../data/lastmod.mjs';
import { getLivePost } from '../../lib/content';
import { p11, contractingWhatIsAnswer, contractingVsRosterAnswer, contractingSeeBeforeSignAnswer } from '../../data/directAnswers';
const roster = await getLivePost('roster-add-vs-full-enrollment');
const contractingTitle = 'Payer contracting for telehealth, in your name';
const contractingDescription = 'Payer contracting for telehealth: commercial and Medicaid payer enrollment in your name. Own your payer contracts, with published rates.';
const contractingGuideAnswer = 'Payer contracting for telehealth is getting participation agreements in the care company\'s own entity, for commercial, Medicare, and Medicaid, so the company can bill in-network and keep those contracts if it changes vendors. How long it takes depends on the payer type. The timeline below quotes published standards and does not give one months-to-contract number.';
const contractingArticle = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: contractingTitle,
  description: contractingDescription,
  datePublished: '2026-09-15T00:00:00Z',
  dateModified: `${lastmodFor('/platform/contracting')}T00:00:00Z`,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Anvil' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Anvil', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: absolute('/logo.svg') } },
  mainEntityOfPage: absolute('/platform/contracting'),
  image: absolute('/og-default-v2.png'),
  url: absolute('/platform/contracting'),
};
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is payer contracting for telehealth?', a: contractingWhatIsAnswer },
  { q: 'How do we own our payer contracts?', a: 'We file applications in your PC\'s name. You approve the terms. When a contract is effective, new visits with that payer bill under your entity. The contracts stay with your PC if you ever switch vendors.' },
  { q: 'Do we negotiate with payers ourselves?', a: 'No, unless you want to. Anvil runs contracting for your entity: applications, rate proposals, redlines, and renewals. You approve the terms and you see every rate.' },
  { q: 'How long do contracts for a new entity take?', a: 'Commercial contracts for a new PC commonly land in 4 to 8 months. Medicaid managed care varies by state and can be faster or slower. Your clinicians bill under our PC the whole time, so this never gates revenue.' },
  { q: 'Can we see the rate ladder?', a: 'Yes. Before you sign, the rate lookup shows published ranges by state and code. During contracting you see the proposed and executed rates for your entity. Under rental you see the contracted rate and the platform fee separately.' },
  { q: 'What if a payer panel is closed?', a: 'We tell you before you plan around it. Closed panels are common in behavioral health. We work the exception path and, where it is closed for good, route that payer\'s volume through our PC until it opens.' },
  { q: 'Who holds the contracts after migration?', a: 'Your PC. Anvil manages them under your management services agreement. If you ever switch vendors, the contracts do not move.' },
  { q: 'What are the best payer contracting companies for virtual care startups?', a: p11 },
  { q: 'How is payer contracting for telehealth different from a roster-add?', a: contractingVsRosterAnswer },
  { q: 'What can I see before I sign a telehealth payer contract?', a: contractingSeeBeforeSignAnswer },
];
---
<SubpageLayout
  metaTitle={contractingTitle}
  title="Payer contracting for telehealth, in your name, at rates you can see."
  description={contractingDescription}
  extraJsonLd={[contractingArticle]}
  eyebrow="Platform / Contracting"
  prompt="What is payer contracting for telehealth, and how long does it take to get in-network?"
  promptId="payer-contracting-timeline"
  answer={contractingGuideAnswer}
  updated
  sub="Payer contracting for telehealth puts commercial and Medicaid enrollment in your name. You should never bill under a contract whose rate you cannot see. We show the range before you sign, the executed rate after, and our fee as a separate line."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Platform', path: '/platform' }, { name: 'Contracting', path: '/platform/contracting' }]}
  showCrumbs
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaTitle="Get a contracting plan for your codes and states."
  ctaBody="Tell us what you bill and where. We come back with payer order, expected timelines, and the rate ranges to plan on."
>
  <Section>
    <p class="lede">Payer contracting for telehealth is one of five workstreams on Anvil's <a href="/platform">insurance operations platform</a>.</p>
    <FeatureRow eyebrow="How we contract" title="Rollout order by covered lives, not by whoever answers first" bullets={[
      'Top 3 to 5 payers per state cover most commercial lives.',
      'Medicaid MCO panels worked state by state, with the closed-panel exception path.',
      'Rate proposals built from the published ranges for your codes.',
      'Renewals calendared from day one. The first renewal is where the rate moves.',
      'Every executed rate in your reporting, by payer, by code, by state.',
    ]}>
      <p>Contracting is a sequence, not a batch. We order it so revenue lands earliest where you have clinicians and lives. This is how you <a href="/own-your-contracts">own your payer contracts</a> instead of renting a network. Comparing vendors? See the <a href="/compare/bridge">Bridge alternative</a> fact table. Published ranges are on <a href="/rates">reimbursement rates by state and CPT code</a>.</p>
    </FeatureRow>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:24px">If you are still choosing the entity, read <a href="/own-your-contracts#rent-or-build">whether to rent a PC or build your own</a>. Leaving a vendor starts with <a href="/solutions/switching">switching from a network rental vendor</a>.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="commercial-medicaid" eyebrow="Enrollment" title="Commercial and Medicaid payer enrollment">
    <p class="lede">File commercial and Medicaid payer enrollment in your name so you can bill in-network from the states you serve. Anvil runs that work for your entity so you own your payer contracts, with the rate visible before you sign.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px"><a href="/resources/when-to-accept-insurance">When should a virtual care company accept insurance</a>? Score the seven signals before the first payer application.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px">For <a href="/solutions/behavioral-health">behavioral health payer contracting</a>, closed panels stay on our PC until they open. After a contract is effective, <a href="/platform/rcm">telehealth revenue cycle management</a> runs the claims under the entity that holds it. Our guide to <a href="/resources/what-in-network-nationally-means">what in-network nationally means</a> defines a closed panel and how it differs from a contract.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px">The month by month path for those contracts is <a href="/own-your-contracts">Own your payer contracts vs network rental</a>.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="what-is-payer-contracting" eyebrow="Guide" title="What is payer contracting for telehealth?">
    <p class="lede">{contractingWhatIsAnswer}</p>
    <p class="small muted">Written by Anvil. Sources checked November 17, 2026.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px">Payer contracting for telehealth is the work of getting participation agreements in the care company's own entity, for commercial, Medicare, and Medicaid, so the company can bill in-network and keep those contracts if it changes vendors.</p>
    <h3>What a participation agreement is</h3>
    <p>A participation agreement is the contract a payer signs with the entity that will bill it. The agreement stays with that entity.</p>
    <h3>How the work is ordered</h3>
    <p>Commercial participation agreements, then Medicare enrollment, then state Medicaid enrollment, then Medicaid MCO panels. State enrollment comes before an MCO panel. That is a sequence of applications, not a count of months.</p>
    <p>A roster-add is the other path: a clinician is added to a contract that already exists. <a href="#roster-vs-contract">How that differs</a> is below. <a href="#rate-before-you-sign">What you can see before you sign</a> is the published range, then the proposed rate, then the executed rate.</p>
    <p>A contract, a panel, and a roster are defined in <a href="/resources/what-in-network-nationally-means">what in-network nationally means</a>.{roster && <> <a href={`/resources/${roster.id}`}>Roster-add versus full enrollment</a> separates those two steps.</>}</p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="in-network-timeline" eyebrow="Timeline" title="How long does it take to get in-network?">
    <p class="lede">These rows are published processing standards, not Anvil averages. Medicaid enrollment is set state by state. An MCO panel is a separate step after state enrollment. This table does not list an Anvil months-to-contract figure.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Payer type</th><th>What the clock measures</th><th>Published timeframe</th><th>Source</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Medicare, internet PECOS</td>
            <td>First Coast Service Options publishes the CMS processing standard for internet-based PECOS initial enrollment and change of information. The clock starts at receipt. It is not an effective date for any one application.</td>
            <td>No site visit, development, or fingerprinting: 95% within 15 calendar days of receipt, and 100% within 50 calendar days. With a site visit, development, or fingerprinting: 95% within 50 calendar days, and 100% within 85.</td>
            <td><a href="https://medicare.fcso.com/enrollment/cms-855-enrollment-application-processing-timeframes" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FCSO CMS-855 timeframes</a>, page dated February 18, 2026, checked November 17, 2026.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Medicare, paper CMS-855</td>
            <td>The same page, for paper initial enrollments, change of information, and opt-out affidavits. Receipt to contractor completion. Not an effective date.</td>
            <td>No site visit, development, or fingerprinting: 95% within 30 calendar days of receipt, and 100% within 65. With a site visit, development, or fingerprinting: 95% within 65 calendar days, and 100% within 100.</td>
            <td><a href="https://medicare.fcso.com/enrollment/cms-855-enrollment-application-processing-timeframes" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FCSO CMS-855 timeframes</a>, page dated February 18, 2026, checked November 17, 2026.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>State Medicaid enrollment, North Carolina example</td>
            <td>NCTracks enrollment and re-enrollment turnaround for clean, complete electronic applications. Not a national Medicaid average.</td>
            <td>The page showed 15.9 days (July), 16.94 days (August), and 22.06 days (September).</td>
            <td><a href="https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/providers/provider-enrollment" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NC Medicaid provider enrollment</a>, page last modified October 1, 2026, checked November 17, 2026.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Commercial, multi-state contracts</td>
            <td>What Bridge published about time to secure multi-state contracts. Not an Anvil figure, and not a national standard. No official national processing standard is cited here.</td>
            <td>6 to 12 months, and longer if starting from scratch, as that page states it.</td>
            <td><a href="https://www.usebridge.com/resources/build-vs-partner" rel="noopener" target="_blank">usebridge.com, build vs partner</a>, checked November 17, 2026.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Arizona insurer credentialing</td>
            <td>Arizona Revised Statutes 20-3453, a state credentialing clock for a health insurer after a complete application. Not a national average, and not a filing instruction.</td>
            <td>Conclude credentialing within 60 calendar days, and load the applicant into the billing system within 30 calendar days. If a licensed facility has a delegated credentialing agreement, the 60-day and 30-day clocks in subsection A do not apply, and loading is within 10 calendar days after the insurer receives a roster of demographic changes.</td>
            <td><a href="https://www.azleg.gov/ars/20/03453.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Arizona Revised Statutes 20-3453</a>, checked November 17, 2026.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Virginia carrier credentialing</td>
            <td>Virginia Code 38.2-3407.10:1, a state clock for a carrier to approve or deny a new provider applicant. Not a national average, and not a filing instruction.</td>
            <td>Approve or deny within 60 days of a completed application. Beginning January 1, 2024, the application is deemed complete within 30 days of receipt unless the carrier notices that it is not complete.</td>
            <td><a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title38.2/chapter34/section38.2-3407.10:1/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Virginia Code 38.2-3407.10:1</a>, checked November 17, 2026.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:16px">The FCSO page lists situations that stop the Medicare processing clock. When fingerprints are required, the clock stops when the request is issued and resumes when the results are received. The page attributes the timeframes to CMS and does not name a Program Integrity Manual section, so this table does not add one.</p>
    <p>NCQA's public blog, dated November 19, 2024, says NCQA requires recredentialing every three years and that it shortened the verification time frame. It does not state a 120-day clock, so this table does not list one. <a href="https://www.ncqa.org/blog/ncqas-credentialing-standards-ensure-safety-and-integrity-of-practitioner-networks/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NCQA credentialing standards blog</a>, checked November 17, 2026.</p>
    <p>For intake, application bounces, and follow-up, see <a href="/resources/credentialing-timeline-benchmark-by-payer">the credentialing timeline guide</a>. The day counts in that guide are not a source for this table.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="roster-vs-contract" eyebrow="Roster-add vs a contract" title="How is payer contracting for telehealth different from a roster-add?">
    <p class="lede">{contractingVsRosterAnswer}</p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="rate-before-you-sign" eyebrow="The rate" title="What can I see before I sign a telehealth payer contract?">
    <p class="lede">{contractingSeeBeforeSignAnswer} The top 3 to 5 commercial payers in a state cover most commercial lives. Medicaid MCO panels come state by state. A closed panel stays on our PC until it opens.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section eyebrow="Coverage" title="Where you can bill on our PC today, and where we can contract yours" tight>
    <CoverageMap />
  </Section>
  <Section tight>
    <RateLookup compact id="rl-contracting" />
  </Section>
</SubpageLayout>
